WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.055
An ordered pair gives two instructions: move horizontally to the first coordinate, then vertically to the second

00:00:07.055 --> 00:00:12.926
The point three, two lies three units right of the origin and two units up

00:00:12.926 --> 00:00:15.667
Reversing the order usually changes the point.

00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:20.115
Negative coordinates describe direction as well as size

00:00:20.115 --> 00:00:24.837
Negative two, one means move two units left, then one unit up

00:00:24.837 --> 00:00:31.133
Both coordinates refer to the same origin and the marked scale, not to the previous point.

00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:35.865
To compare the two points, subtract corresponding coordinates

00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:43.586
From negative two, one to three, two, the horizontal change is five and the vertical change is one

00:00:43.586 --> 00:00:48.733
Differences describe movement between points even though neither point is the origin.

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:55.686
A horizontal line contains every point with the same vertical coordinate

00:00:55.686 --> 00:01:02.881
The equation y equals two therefore allows many x values while keeping height fixed

00:01:02.881 --> 00:01:08.533
Coordinates connect individual locations with entire geometric relationships described by equations.
